Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Optional: Configure Relativity Connection Symbol", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9775176800467176`*^9, 3.977517685457151*^9}, {
  3.977644427456564*^9, 3.9776444329596653`*^9}, {3.978358022660828*^9, 
  3.9783580250320253`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"fd562242-cb5f-4257-94c4-\
9c3dcffe6af8"],

Cell["\<\
To properly compose covariant derivatives, \[ScriptCapitalU]\[ScriptCapitalD] \
must know the user\[CloseCurlyQuote]s reserved symbols for non-tensorial \
connection coefficients. In gravitational physics, these force-producing \
connections are called \[CapitalGamma] (capital gamma). Other theories like \
electrodynamics and Yang-Mills that can benefit from \[ScriptCapitalU]\
\[ScriptCapitalD] use other connection symbols. \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.977517690693409*^9, 3.9775178232519913`*^9}, {
  3.97751956997919*^9, 3.9775195720756073`*^9}, {3.9775280931539383`*^9, 
  3.977528124823065*^9}, {3.977535721478539*^9, 3.977535724386561*^9}, {
  3.9776444438816977`*^9, 3.977644712294363*^9}, {3.9777468389285183`*^9, 
  3.977747007351509*^9}, {3.977747054921603*^9, 
  3.9777470563202457`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"860dae44-cbbe-4334-bee0-\
eb8dbe58ad65"],

Cell["\<\
We\[CloseCurlyQuote]re going with \[CapitalGamma] for this \
relativity/gravitation notebook. \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.977517690693409*^9, 3.9775178232519913`*^9}, {
  3.97751956997919*^9, 3.9775195720756073`*^9}, {3.9775280931539383`*^9, 
  3.977528124823065*^9}, {3.977535721478539*^9, 3.977535724386561*^9}, {
  3.9776444438816977`*^9, 3.977644762630517*^9}, {3.977747016178646*^9, 
  3.97774702870907*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"6412f41d-f02b-4a24-b243-\
19b7651c936f"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"SetConnection", "[", "\[CapitalGamma]", "]"}], ";"}], 
  "\n"}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9775137220895033`*^9, 3.977513731036991*^9}, {
   3.9775149251253233`*^9, 3.9775149333295393`*^9}, 3.9775150554680758`*^9, 
   3.977517830816463*^9},
 CellLabel->"In[8]:=",ExpressionUUID->"2b33fb69-f8c2-48fe-a7a1-6d53867f0567"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["All Physics is Gauge Theory", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978638647735394*^9, 3.9786386508345337`*^9}, {
  3.978641316636702*^9, 3.978641319674756*^9}, {3.978737839482726*^9, 
  3.9787378449477777`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"7c1aaaaf-5aaa-4d52-a80b-\
964e26b46f48"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A physics student usually first encounters the term \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]gauge\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] in classical \
electrodynamics. However, the same notion pertains even in elementary physics \
in myriad ways. For an elementary example, consider measuring the altitude of \
an Earth satellite flying in a stable, circular, free-fall orbit. If we \
measure altitude with respect to mean sea level, the satellite seems to have \
a constant altitude, Earth\[CloseCurlyQuote]s oblateness aside. However, if \
we measure altitude with respect to the irregular surface of the Earth, the \
satellite\[CloseCurlyQuote]s altitude varies madly up and down. To naive \
observers, unaware of their poor choice of coordinate system, the satellite \
appears to suffer inexplicable forces pushing it up and down! However, these ",
 "apparent",
 " forces arise merely from the choice of ",
 StyleBox["gauge",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ", that is, the definition of a measurement reference frame. Here, the term \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]gauge\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] fits entirely with its \
ordinary meaning, say the arbitrary origin of an altimeter or the width of a \
rail line, etc."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9786386648875933`*^9, 3.978639147971436*^9}, {
  3.9786401089109507`*^9, 3.97864013021977*^9}, {3.978640191966693*^9, 
  3.978640516557682*^9}, {3.978641281651569*^9, 3.9786412836572237`*^9}, {
  3.978737855552356*^9, 3.9787379905163193`*^9}, {3.9787380221754427`*^9, 
  3.978738036037778*^9}, {3.9787889122095747`*^9, 3.978789038628954*^9}, {
  3.978789131640423*^9, 3.978789330099185*^9}, {3.978789377753706*^9, 
  3.978789388367732*^9}, {3.978789489346162*^9, 3.9787895396860113`*^9}, {
  3.9787897001908827`*^9, 
  3.978789714267542*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"3b439049-7cf4-4a34-bc54-\
20d77b2ca349"],

Cell["\<\
A similar things happens in rotating reference frames. If we measure \
positions and momenta of free particles with respect to an inertial lab \
frame, we see no forces. If, however, we choose to measure from a rotating \
frame, we see centrifugal and Coriolis forces, arising merely from the choice \
of gauge, i.e., reference frame.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978639090934486*^9, 3.9786390959409924`*^9}, {
  3.9786391581020203`*^9, 3.9786392916461267`*^9}, {3.978639339607583*^9, 
  3.978639341057925*^9}, {3.978639843797641*^9, 3.978639883871458*^9}, {
  3.978640558948505*^9, 3.978640564055111*^9}, {3.978738055466886*^9, 
  3.978738056188883*^9}, {3.978789393960122*^9, 
  3.978789410670973*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"26cac6e5-0520-4bb4-8d59-\
23cca7409c14"],

Cell["\<\
Resolving these conundrums concisely requires gauge theory, totally pertinent \
to classical mechanics, but too heavy for introductory physics. That\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]s why physics students don\[CloseCurlyQuote]t study gauge \
until they get into electrodynamics, where it\[CloseCurlyQuote]s some \
abstract, arbitrary field added to the vector potential, with no conceivable \
relationship to the ordinary meaning of the word. By that time, it\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]s too late to go back and explain the apparent forces of \
elementary mechanics as arising merely from non-inertial gauge choices. Our \
poor physics students will carry multiple bags, all the way through general \
relativity (GR), until gauge appears again as more abstract wizardry in \
particle physics. They could have packed all their ideas into one piece of \
baggage with a concrete view of gauge as just a choice of reference frame.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978639302944932*^9, 3.978639559880187*^9}, {
   3.978639906097765*^9, 3.97863990678701*^9}, 3.978639968293231*^9, {
   3.9786400579528627`*^9, 3.9786400802416973`*^9}, {3.978640595978593*^9, 
   3.9786408470755568`*^9}, {3.978641348822749*^9, 3.97864137181462*^9}, {
   3.978738070996792*^9, 3.9787380861994247`*^9}, {3.978789438625586*^9, 
   3.978789478159536*^9}, {3.9787895499480867`*^9, 3.97878968026201*^9}, {
   3.978789744864238*^9, 3.978789876297686*^9}, {3.9787914565016108`*^9, 
   3.978791457765832*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"6ceec6ac-1964-4e3a-8308-\
bc09bb1272c4"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["All forces, apparent and real, do, in fact, arise from choice of \
gauge",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". For the \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]real\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] forces, we \
can\[CloseCurlyQuote]t choose a gauge that gets rid of them, as we could do \
in mechanics with inertial frames. But the ",
 StyleBox["symmetries",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " of gauge choices drive forces, real and apparent, via Noether\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]s theorem, which really should be the travel sticker on our \
baggage and the visa stamps in our passports as we journey through all of \
physics. "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978640894250175*^9, 3.978641074879751*^9}, {
  3.978641407380624*^9, 3.9786414732143106`*^9}, {3.9786420394434547`*^9, 
  3.978642039792768*^9}, {3.9787381300235453`*^9, 3.978738138526342*^9}, {
  3.978789896455791*^9, 
  3.978789928750749*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"38b64028-6be8-42fb-8046-\
17cfbe0c10fc"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Gauge Forces as Connections", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9786365673927803`*^9, 3.9786365728569317`*^9}, {
  3.978637906305097*^9, 3.978637911784799*^9}, {3.978641629594967*^9, 
  3.978641632842622*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"de2e5ba2-e25c-4f93-bcd4-\
a727b1f176d3"],

Cell["\<\
We\[CloseCurlyQuote]re lucky. We\[CloseCurlyQuote]ve already studied \
covariant derivatives and connections in this series of notebooks, \
specifically in relativity, special and general. We\[CloseCurlyQuote]ve \
repeatedly seen that we can and should recast these ideas in terms of gauge \
theory. Now is the time, with the incandescent clarity that gauge is no more \
nor less than choice of reference frame in a generalized sense. We\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]ve seen that geometrical frame invariance, now geometrical \
gauge invariance, forces the \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]discovery\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] of covariant derivative and connection. We\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]ve seen that such connections drive equations of motion, \
say for Schwarzschild orbits. We\[CloseCurlyQuote]re now positioned to \
explore the other forces of physics in the same terms.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978641572817196*^9, 3.978641680574541*^9}, {
  3.9786417137944403`*^9, 3.9786418997181177`*^9}, {3.978641933868432*^9, 
  3.978641951159326*^9}, {3.978738177896667*^9, 3.9787382112497663`*^9}, {
  3.978789968088002*^9, 
  3.9787900903971*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"1a069641-6cc6-4768-99db-9d7dbb563bb2"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Back to our physics students, with lighter baggage, encountering gauge \
again, ",
 StyleBox["not",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " for the first time, in classical electrodynamics. One realizes that the \
gradient of any differentiable scalar field \[CapitalLambda], called a gauge, \
can be added to the vector potential without altering the physics:"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978636576303603*^9, 3.9786366785799294`*^9}, {
   3.978636723696795*^9, 3.978636870996152*^9}, {3.978636910194687*^9, 
   3.978636921670682*^9}, {3.9786370048263483`*^9, 3.9786370849609137`*^9}, 
   3.978637277938579*^9, {3.9786380563234453`*^9, 3.978638089888694*^9}, {
   3.978641485319695*^9, 3.9786415366166553`*^9}, {3.9786419540622797`*^9, 
   3.9786419851870832`*^9}, {3.978738229613509*^9, 3.978738232163897*^9}, {
   3.978738275893861*^9, 3.9787382803704367`*^9}, {3.978790103133443*^9, 
   3.978790176728155*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a613ee36-0c11-41f8-a3ac-\
7ed05a5d6d67"],

Cell[BoxData[
 FormBox[
  RowBox[{
   SubscriptBox[
    OverscriptBox["A", "_"], 
    RowBox[{"\[VeryThinSpace]", "\[Mu]"}]], "=", 
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["A", 
     RowBox[{"\[VeryThinSpace]", "\[Mu]"}]], "+", 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["\[PartialD]", "\[Mu]"], 
     RowBox[{"\[CapitalLambda]", "(", 
      SuperscriptBox["x", "\[Nu]"], ")"}]}]}]}], 
  TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormulaNumbered",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978637090481073*^9, 3.978637121098152*^9}, {
  3.9786372899247313`*^9, 3.978637455330873*^9}, {3.978637578634162*^9, 
  3.978637584593306*^9}, {3.978638122624658*^9, 
  3.978638130156509*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"32584ea5-15fb-48bb-b029-\
3d8673cac545"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In what sense is \[CapitalLambda] the choice of a reference frame? To \
resolve this conundrum, we have to see that electrodynamical gauge is also \
presented as ",
 StyleBox["phase invariance",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ", a factor of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["\[ExponentialE]", 
    RowBox[{" ", 
     RowBox[{"i", "\[VeryThinSpace]", "\[CurlyPhi]"}]}]], TraditionalForm]],
  ExpressionUUID->"05497215-45aa-42e1-b0a6-22d1a630604b"],
 " that can be multiplied into a charged matter field (classical) or into a \
Dirac wave function (quantum) without altering the physics. Spoiler: choose \
phase in an abstract kind of reference frame; this is the ",
 StyleBox["primary",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " principle of electrodynamical gauge as a coordinate frame. Gradient added \
to vector potential is the necessary ",
 StyleBox["mechanism",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ", the \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]induced \
transformation,\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] required to uphold that principle. \
Here\[CloseCurlyQuote]s how that works."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978636576303603*^9, 3.9786366785799294`*^9}, {
  3.978636723696795*^9, 3.978636870996152*^9}, {3.978636910194687*^9, 
  3.978636921670682*^9}, {3.9786370048263483`*^9, 3.978637074091062*^9}, {
  3.9786374735329943`*^9, 3.97863749900111*^9}, {3.9786420889624434`*^9, 
  3.978642183847044*^9}, {3.978645450479156*^9, 3.978645504372199*^9}, {
  3.978738291981337*^9, 3.978738457947143*^9}, {3.978790183027944*^9, 
  3.9787902062151537`*^9}, {3.978790241679438*^9, 
  3.978790364497869*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"f0ddc887-eb41-41eb-9130-\
415710d079c6"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Change a matter field \[Psi] by rotating its phase (gauge) by an arbitrary \
(but smoothly varying) amount, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"\[CapitalLambda]", "(", 
    SuperscriptBox["x", "\[Nu]"], ")"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "8887eb8f-8e50-4c43-bc2b-5fa6ed172500"],
 ", at every point ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["x", "\[Nu]"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "148377dc-a2d4-4c2d-ad37-7d9e8c50c6fa"],
 ":"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978637538672182*^9, 3.978637755565897*^9}, {
  3.978642236477104*^9, 3.978642246171332*^9}, {3.9787903747991543`*^9, 
  3.978790376943686*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"bbd91da1-9c66-4a41-b4ca-\
51cbc1f36766"],

Cell[BoxData[
 FormBox[
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    OverscriptBox["\[Psi]", "_"], "(", 
    SuperscriptBox["x", "\[Nu]"], ")"}], "=", 
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox["\[ExponentialE]", 
     RowBox[{"\[VeryThinSpace]", 
      RowBox[{"i", "\[VeryThinSpace]", 
       RowBox[{"\[CapitalLambda]", "(", 
        SuperscriptBox["x", "\[Nu]"], ")"}]}]}]], "\[Psi]", 
    RowBox[{"(", 
     SuperscriptBox["x", "\[Nu]"], ")"}]}]}], 
  TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormulaNumbered",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978637538672182*^9, 3.97863778411996*^9}, {
  3.9786379712287197`*^9, 
  3.9786379796646557`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"2699533f-e0e2-4931-b108-\
e52ba5a0a837"],

Cell["\<\
This is just like choosing a coordinate system with an arbitrary origin, \
locally, at each point, for measuring the phase of the matter fields.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978642193583212*^9, 3.978642224078884*^9}, {
  3.9787384986624413`*^9, 3.978738566270625*^9}, {3.978790924023786*^9, 
  3.978790949934161*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"2f9bbb0b-d116-44fa-86dd-\
f610d75178b3"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Take the ordinary partial derivative, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[PartialD]", "\[Mu]"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "dbe210c8-941f-4a8e-b180-8f7245a144d6"],
 ", of this new field ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   OverscriptBox["\[Psi]", "_"], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "cb83cb2a-980f-4291-9ce1-09b15724f99f"],
 ". The ordinary product rule spits out a \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]garbage term\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] because ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["\[CapitalLambda]", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "140f5d75-b68e-465c-9c4e-2609eda551b2"],
 " varies with position:"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978637795314996*^9, 3.9786378832705383`*^9}, {
  3.978638180667034*^9, 3.9786381881906633`*^9}, {3.978642259537039*^9, 
  3.978642260939106*^9}, {3.978738583002688*^9, 3.978738586607667*^9}, {
  3.978790411450951*^9, 
  3.9787904159652767`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"d709d7b9-a0b8-444c-a891-\
799112cdd05f"],

Cell[BoxData[{
 FormBox[
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["\[PartialD]", "\[Mu]"], 
     RowBox[{
      OverscriptBox["\[Psi]", "_"], "(", 
      SuperscriptBox["x", "\[Nu]"], ")"}]}], "=", 
    RowBox[{
     SuperscriptBox["\[ExponentialE]", 
      RowBox[{"\[VeryThinSpace]", 
       RowBox[{"i", "\[VeryThinSpace]", 
        RowBox[{"\[CapitalLambda]", "(", 
         SuperscriptBox["x", "\[Nu]"], ")"}]}]}]], "  ", 
     RowBox[{"(", "  ", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{
        SubscriptBox["\[PartialD]", "\[Mu]"], 
        RowBox[{"\[Psi]", "(", 
         SuperscriptBox["x", "\[Nu]"], ")"}]}], "+", 
       RowBox[{"i", 
        RowBox[{"(", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{
           SubscriptBox["\[PartialD]", "\[Mu]"], "\[CapitalLambda]"}], 
          RowBox[{"(", 
           SuperscriptBox["x", "\[Nu]"], ")"}]}], ")"}], 
        RowBox[{"\[Psi]", "(", 
         SuperscriptBox["x", "\[Nu]"], ")"}]}]}], "  ", ")"}]}]}], ",", " ", 
   "or", ",", " ", "abbreviating"}], TraditionalForm], "\[LineSeparator]", 
 FormBox[
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["\[PartialD]", "\[Mu]"], 
    OverscriptBox["\[Psi]", "_"]}], "=", 
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox["\[ExponentialE]", 
     RowBox[{"\[VeryThinSpace]", 
      RowBox[{"i", "\[VeryThinSpace]", "\[CapitalLambda]"}]}]], "  ", 
    RowBox[{"(", "  ", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{
       SubscriptBox["\[PartialD]", "\[Mu]"], "\[Psi]"}], "+", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"i", "(", 
        RowBox[{
         SubscriptBox["\[PartialD]", "\[Mu]"], "\[CapitalLambda]"}], ")"}], 
       "\[Psi]"}]}], "  ", ")"}]}]}], 
  TraditionalForm]}], "DisplayFormulaNumbered",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978637887868793*^9, 3.9786378947514553`*^9}, {
  3.978637927473442*^9, 3.9786379497151003`*^9}, {3.978637985585307*^9, 
  3.9786380459954576`*^9}, {3.978638197487301*^9, 3.97863825659056*^9}, {
  3.978790463257308*^9, 3.978790502671747*^9}, {3.978790553765525*^9, 
  3.978790557365933*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"62e7a310-9def-4de0-b41b-\
525e0f7c85ce"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Sound familiar? This is exactly what happened in relativity. The \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]garbage terms\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] drove our \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]discovery\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] of covariant \
derivative and connection coefficients. Here, the extra term ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"i", "(", 
     RowBox[{
      SubscriptBox["\[PartialD]", "\[Mu]"], "\[CapitalLambda]"}], ")"}], 
    "\[Psi]"}], TraditionalForm]],
  CellChangeTimes->{{3.978637887868793*^9, 3.9786378947514553`*^9}, {
   3.978637927473442*^9, 3.9786379497151003`*^9}, {3.978637985585307*^9, 
   3.9786380459954576`*^9}, {3.978638197487301*^9, 3.97863825659056*^9}},
  ExpressionUUID->"2d5d94d4-52a6-4301-98a1-27bff23628c1"],
 " ruins the rest of the physics. As before, we find that the ordinary \
derivative is not covariant: it doesn\[CloseCurlyQuote]t survive changes in \
spacetime coordinate system from ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox["x", "\[Nu]"], "->", 
    SuperscriptBox["x", 
     RowBox[{"\[Nu]", "'"}]]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "410f97c0-b2bb-4b4d-90f5-d852ea8bfd74"],
 "."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978638433441883*^9, 3.978638454013837*^9}, {
  3.978638510142865*^9, 3.978638525977479*^9}, {3.978638574219385*^9, 
  3.9786385939606123`*^9}, {3.9786423189988194`*^9, 3.978642326146699*^9}, {
  3.978790523634274*^9, 3.97879052765222*^9}, {3.978790577036037*^9, 
  3.978790824183269*^9}, {3.978790987080504*^9, 
  3.978790987746471*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"b44631bb-d396-4767-9ec4-\
dc3c65c2de71"],

Cell["\<\
With electrodynamics, compensating for the coordinate changes is easier. In \
GR, the gauge was the geometry itself, so compensating for the effect of \
coordinate change required 4D index manipulation. The electrodynamical \
connection is a one-index object because the electrodynamical gauge field is \
one-dimensional. \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978643141459524*^9, 3.9786431828556843`*^9}, {
  3.978738641173134*^9, 3.9787386532797832`*^9}, {3.9787908465552607`*^9, 
  3.978790856317902*^9}, {3.978791011986392*^9, 
  3.9787911783544617`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"3414dda7-eeae-4408-b9c8-\
65d7bb7ac439"],

Cell["\<\
To clean up the electrodynamical gauge garbage, we need a correction term in \
our covariant derivative, just as we did with geometry and GR:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978643216420027*^9, 3.978643271965823*^9}, {
  3.978791185639865*^9, 
  3.978791190604082*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"c3bdd501-64cd-4593-9110-\
51b97f3a3483"],

Cell[BoxData[
 FormBox[
  RowBox[{
   SubscriptBox["D", "\[Mu]"], "\[Congruent]", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["\[PartialD]", "\[Mu]"], 
     RowBox[{"-", "i"}]}], "\[VeryThinSpace]", 
    SubscriptBox["A", "\[Mu]"]}]}], 
  TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormulaNumbered",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978643276164227*^9, 3.9786432889746*^9}, {
  3.978643322108322*^9, 3.978643337180196*^9}, {3.978643475279353*^9, 
  3.978643475281219*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"4b3596e5-775e-4090-a055-\
8eaa17ef6d01"],

Cell["\<\
Yes, the connection is the vector potential, hence it drives forces! \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9786433489592524`*^9, 3.978643399831469*^9}, {
  3.978643451260046*^9, 3.9786434570432673`*^9}, {3.978738674339534*^9, 
  3.9787386751608458`*^9}, {3.978791202722198*^9, 3.978791212226269*^9}},
 FontWeight->"Bold",ExpressionUUID->"4bf37e51-2ef4-47e2-827e-789c2120b0a8"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "For the garbage terms to cancel out exactly, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["A", "\[Mu]"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "cf9ccc92-131a-4340-9235-8ec665407f64"],
 " must transform as we learned in the first time in school, in Equation 1:"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9786433489592524`*^9, 3.978643399831469*^9}, {
  3.978643451260046*^9, 3.9786434570432673`*^9}, {3.978738674339534*^9, 
  3.9787386751608458`*^9}, {3.978791202722198*^9, 
  3.978791249452368*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a7c233ac-ac42-49e6-a9c8-\
3607a8c9035f"],

Cell[BoxData[
 FormBox[
  RowBox[{
   SubscriptBox[
    OverscriptBox["A", "_"], 
    RowBox[{"\[VeryThinSpace]", "\[Mu]"}]], "=", 
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["A", 
     RowBox[{"\[VeryThinSpace]", "\[Mu]"}]], "+", 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["\[PartialD]", "\[Mu]"], "\[CapitalLambda]"}]}]}], 
  TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormulaNumbered",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978637090481073*^9, 3.978637121098152*^9}, {
  3.9786372899247313`*^9, 3.978637455330873*^9}, {3.978637578634162*^9, 
  3.978637584593306*^9}, {3.978638122624658*^9, 3.978638130156509*^9}, {
  3.9786434831358967`*^9, 
  3.978643484399239*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"8246d647-b649-43c2-88f5-\
751f17de6404"],

Cell["Now we have ", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978643488286076*^9, 3.9786434909815903`*^9}, {
  3.978791254151824*^9, 
  3.978791255677359*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"da41081c-7849-4055-973c-\
2f4774d27dcb"],

Cell[BoxData[
 FormBox[
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["D", "\[Mu]"], "\[Psi]"}], "\[Congruent]", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["\[PartialD]", "\[Mu]"], "\[Psi]"}], "-", 
    RowBox[{"i", "\[VeryThinSpace]", 
     SubscriptBox["A", "\[Mu]"], "\[Psi]"}]}]}], 
  TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormulaNumbered",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9786435032056303`*^9, 
  3.978643606837364*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"024e8a50-8217-4ff4-b17b-\
0b0e6867c4b7"],

Cell["and", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978643593775935*^9, 
  3.978643594327322*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"32719714-8e0a-4b65-8a22-\
07f3d69ea5fc"],

Cell[BoxData[{
 FormBox[
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["D", "\[Mu]"], 
    OverscriptBox["\[Psi]", "_"]}], "=", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{
      SubscriptBox["\[PartialD]", "\[Mu]"], 
      OverscriptBox["\[Psi]", "_"]}], "-", 
     RowBox[{"i", "\[VeryThinSpace]", 
      SubscriptBox[
       OverscriptBox["A", "_"], "\[Mu]"], 
      OverscriptBox["\[Psi]", "_"]}]}], "=", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{
      SuperscriptBox["\[ExponentialE]", 
       RowBox[{"\[VeryThinSpace]", 
        RowBox[{"i", "\[VeryThinSpace]", "\[CapitalLambda]"}]}]], " ", 
      RowBox[{"(", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{
         SubscriptBox["\[PartialD]", "\[Mu]"], "\[Psi]"}], "+", 
        RowBox[{"i", 
         RowBox[{"(", 
          RowBox[{
           SubscriptBox["\[PartialD]", "\[Mu]"], "\[CapitalLambda]"}], ")"}], 
         "\[Psi]"}]}], ")"}]}], "-", 
     RowBox[{"i", "\[VeryThinSpace]", 
      SubscriptBox[
       OverscriptBox["A", "_"], "\[Mu]"], 
      OverscriptBox["\[Psi]", "_"]}]}]}]}], TraditionalForm], "\[LineSeparator]", 
 FormBox[
  RowBox[{"=", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     SuperscriptBox["\[ExponentialE]", 
      RowBox[{"\[VeryThinSpace]", 
       RowBox[{"i", "\[VeryThinSpace]", "\[CapitalLambda]"}]}]], " ", 
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{
        SubscriptBox["\[PartialD]", "\[Mu]"], "\[Psi]"}], "+", 
       RowBox[{"i", 
        RowBox[{"(", 
         RowBox[{
          SubscriptBox["\[PartialD]", "\[Mu]"], "\[CapitalLambda]"}], ")"}], 
        "\[Psi]"}]}], ")"}]}], "-", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"i", "(", 
      RowBox[{
       SubscriptBox["A", 
        RowBox[{"\[VeryThinSpace]", "\[Mu]"}]], "+", 
       RowBox[{
        SubscriptBox["\[PartialD]", "\[Mu]"], "\[CapitalLambda]"}]}], ")"}], 
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{
       SuperscriptBox["\[ExponentialE]", 
        RowBox[{"\[VeryThinSpace]", 
         RowBox[{"i", "\[VeryThinSpace]", "\[CapitalLambda]"}]}]], "\[Psi]"}],
       ")"}]}]}]}], TraditionalForm], "\[LineSeparator]", 
 FormBox[
  RowBox[{"=", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     SuperscriptBox["\[ExponentialE]", 
      RowBox[{"\[VeryThinSpace]", 
       RowBox[{"i", "\[VeryThinSpace]", "\[CapitalLambda]"}]}]], " ", 
     RowBox[{
      SubscriptBox["\[PartialD]", "\[Mu]"], "\[Psi]"}]}], 
    StyleBox["+",
     FontVariations->{"StrikeThrough"->True}], 
    StyleBox[
     RowBox[{
      SuperscriptBox["\[ExponentialE]", 
       RowBox[{"\[VeryThinSpace]", 
        RowBox[{"i", "\[VeryThinSpace]", "\[CapitalLambda]"}]}]], "i", 
      RowBox[{"(", 
       RowBox[{
        SubscriptBox["\[PartialD]", "\[Mu]"], "\[CapitalLambda]"}], ")"}], 
      "\[Psi]"}],
     FontVariations->{"StrikeThrough"->True}], "-", 
    RowBox[{
     SuperscriptBox["\[ExponentialE]", 
      RowBox[{"\[VeryThinSpace]", 
       RowBox[{"i", "\[VeryThinSpace]", "\[CapitalLambda]"}]}]], "i", "\[VeryThinSpace]", 
     SubscriptBox["A", 
      RowBox[{"\[VeryThinSpace]", "\[Mu]"}]], "\[Psi]"}], 
    StyleBox["-",
     FontVariations->{"StrikeThrough"->True}], 
    StyleBox[
     RowBox[{
      SuperscriptBox["\[ExponentialE]", 
       RowBox[{"\[VeryThinSpace]", 
        RowBox[{"i", "\[VeryThinSpace]", "\[CapitalLambda]"}]}]], "i", 
      RowBox[{"(", 
       RowBox[{
        SubscriptBox["\[PartialD]", "\[Mu]"], "\[CapitalLambda]"}], ")"}], 
      "\[Psi]"}],
     FontVariations->{"StrikeThrough"->True}]}]}], TraditionalForm], "\[LineSeparator]", 
 FormBox[
  RowBox[{"=", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     SuperscriptBox["\[ExponentialE]", 
      RowBox[{"\[VeryThinSpace]", 
       RowBox[{"i", "\[VeryThinSpace]", "\[CapitalLambda]"}]}]], " ", 
     RowBox[{
      SubscriptBox["\[PartialD]", "\[Mu]"], "\[Psi]"}]}], "-", 
    RowBox[{
     SuperscriptBox["\[ExponentialE]", 
      RowBox[{"\[VeryThinSpace]", 
       RowBox[{"i", "\[VeryThinSpace]", "\[CapitalLambda]"}]}]], "i", "\[VeryThinSpace]", 
     SubscriptBox["A", 
      RowBox[{"\[VeryThinSpace]", "\[Mu]"}]], "\[Psi]"}]}]}], 
  TraditionalForm], "\[LineSeparator]", 
 FormBox[
  RowBox[{"=", 
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox["\[ExponentialE]", 
     RowBox[{"\[VeryThinSpace]", 
      RowBox[{"i", "\[VeryThinSpace]", "\[CapitalLambda]"}]}]], "(", " ", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{
      SubscriptBox["\[PartialD]", "\[Mu]"], "\[Psi]"}], "-", 
     RowBox[{"i", "\[VeryThinSpace]", 
      SubscriptBox["A", 
       RowBox[{"\[VeryThinSpace]", "\[Mu]"}]], "\[Psi]"}]}], ")"}]}], 
  TraditionalForm]}], "DisplayFormulaNumbered",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9786436192725773`*^9, 3.9786437727905197`*^9}, {
  3.978643806573044*^9, 3.978643946308076*^9}, {3.978644002327197*^9, 
  3.978644220022155*^9}, {3.978644291740561*^9, 3.9786443538434763`*^9}, {
  3.978644388511799*^9, 
  3.978644510127365*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a5eab264-474f-4226-a0ae-\
4d3f337c5c42"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "transforming covariantly with respect to the gauge choice ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["\[ExponentialE]", 
    RowBox[{"\[VeryThinSpace]", 
     RowBox[{"i", "\[VeryThinSpace]", "\[CapitalLambda]"}]}]], 
   TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->"b4f243b0-2c58-4140-977d-0486ffe6a72b"],
 ", exactly, if metaphorically to geometrical gauge in GR, covariant with its \
down index."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.97864452629532*^9, 3.9786446238462*^9}, 
   3.978738689142243*^9, {3.978738724694315*^9, 3.978738784872237*^9}, {
   3.978791265368784*^9, 
   3.9787913061211567`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"6e85698d-954f-469b-848a-\
601c91c29c9b"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["We now see why it\[CloseCurlyQuote]s a good idea, after all, to \
call the covariant derivative ",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "covariant",
 StyleBox[". Such honors the metaphor everywhere without being exactly \
correct in GR, a fact we dismissed as \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]historical\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] in an earlier notebook.",
  FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978644642417985*^9, 3.978644753146748*^9}, {
  3.978645552948409*^9, 3.978645554732916*^9}, {3.978738701834901*^9, 
  3.978738704297278*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"3eaa4828-6fd0-4989-bab6-\
6e2697261d66"],

Cell["\<\
We\[CloseCurlyQuote]ll write some new functions for the Relativity Toolkit to \
automate the calculation above, after just one last philosophical section, \
which bridges our old material for GR more deeply to the new material on \
gauges in general.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.97864452629532*^9, 3.978644608759818*^9}, {
  3.9786447582836847`*^9, 3.978644803628751*^9}, {3.9786458816287622`*^9, 
  3.978645931893115*^9}, {3.978646349194942*^9, 3.978646350719452*^9}, {
  3.978791323640992*^9, 3.9787913558959093`*^9}, {3.978791495646101*^9, 
  3.9787915191520576`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"ac12e704-4f80-48a5-b9da-\
4e11ea15a54f"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Covariant Derivatives, Revisited", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978358251530368*^9, 
  3.978358274502495*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"7f6b77f4-b0fc-4a67-b903-\
969955fdadc1"],

Cell["\<\
In GR, we learned to take covariant derivatives of tensors that inhabit the \
tangent and cotangent tensor spaces of curved manifolds. The canonical \
example is the covariant derivative of a contravariant vector with respect to \
a basis of coordinate functions:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978645943294868*^9, 3.978645997660583*^9}, {
  3.9787915036557417`*^9, 
  3.978791531715695*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"4f010f30-76ff-4c9d-9e45-\
977a9562d116"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"With", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"example", "=", 
      RowBox[{"HoldForm", "[", 
       RowBox[{"CD", "[", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"A", "[", 
          RowBox[{"\[ScriptCapitalU]", "[", "\[Mu]", "]"}], "]"}], ",", 
         RowBox[{"x", "[", 
          RowBox[{"\[ScriptCapitalU]", "[", "\[Nu]", "]"}], "]"}]}], "]"}], 
       "]"}]}], "}"}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    RowBox[{"Echo", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"TensorForm", "@@", "example"}], ",", 
      RowBox[{"example", "\"\<\>\""}]}], "]"}]}], "]"}], ";"}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978358547373548*^9, 3.978358772499927*^9}, {
  3.978359072474093*^9, 3.978359157811211*^9}, {3.9783592724273376`*^9, 
  3.978359272556822*^9}, {3.9783593063094587`*^9, 3.9783593921632147`*^9}, {
  3.978383680431888*^9, 3.978383735895418*^9}},
 CellLabel->"In[10]:=",ExpressionUUID->"78497388-807e-47b1-a2f3-54ab4ce22561"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The connection coefficients \[CapitalGamma] account for the twisting and \
turning of basis vectors in the tangent spaces as the vector ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["A", 
    RowBox[{"\[VeryThinSpace]", "\[Mu]"}]], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "dd3e6e84-9f68-483f-906e-506409ee1c30"],
 " is parallel-transported. In GR, this twisting and turning causes geodesics \
to converge or diverge"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978645943294868*^9, 3.978646017572369*^9}, {
  3.978646073037752*^9, 3.978646096573864*^9}, {3.978791550457979*^9, 
  3.9787915716892157`*^9}, {3.978791607811911*^9, 
  3.978791645252721*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"37424616-b889-4f35-aa46-\
5f5a9d34df7f"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Geodesic deviation is indistinguishable from a force, \
but it\[CloseCurlyQuote]s not an apparent force: we can\[CloseCurlyQuote]t \
get rid of it by a gauge choice.",
 FontSlant->"Italic"]], "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978645943294868*^9, 3.978646017572369*^9}, {
  3.978646073037752*^9, 3.978646096573864*^9}, {3.978791550457979*^9, 
  3.9787915716892157`*^9}, {3.978791607811911*^9, 
  3.9787916688688183`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a0d3e19d-b357-44b9-be80-\
c725fc844522"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The derivative above transforms like a tensor, ensuring physics is ",
 StyleBox["locally Lorentz invariant",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ". As noted in the introduction, this invariance is another application of \
Noether\[CloseCurlyQuote]s theorem. In GR, the conserved quantity arising \
from local Lorentz invariance is ",
 StyleBox["spin current",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ", from symmetries of non-coordinate bases (see Appendix 1)."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978645943294868*^9, 3.97864602352232*^9}, {
  3.97864610862702*^9, 3.9786461676375427`*^9}, {3.978738857168982*^9, 
  3.978738864834247*^9}, {3.9787916844234667`*^9, 
  3.978791699695005*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"9ad9e61c-cea4-41a0-a521-\
e599c7429130"],

Cell["\<\
In this notebook, we consider new spaces \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]attached\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] to points of the manifold like \
wallpaper\[LongDash]gauge fields representing electromagnetism and nuclear \
matter. These wallpaper spaces have their own differential geometries above \
and beyond the geometry of spacetime itself. In gravitation, gauge forces \
arise FROM the geometry. In other theories, gauge forces arise ON the \
geometry.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978645943294868*^9, 3.9786460283023977`*^9}, {
  3.978646177642584*^9, 3.978646180024088*^9}, {3.978646219196919*^9, 
  3.978646221832672*^9}, {3.978738870146287*^9, 3.978738896485022*^9}, {
  3.9787917205922403`*^9, 
  3.978791794129325*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"c95b6d04-962e-46a0-ac47-\
c90b44aaf33b"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["That\[CloseCurlyQuote]s one reason gravitation \
resists unification with the other forces of Nature, as explained more fully \
in the appendices.",
 FontSlant->"Italic"]], "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.97879180288661*^9, 
  3.978791833166924*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"1d99b55f-d1a9-4711-bf92-\
f301849c4261"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "While it is fine for this notebook to envision gauge fields as \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]wallpaper,\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] the rigorous \
mathematical machinery for this is that of ",
 StyleBox["fibre bundles",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " (Appendix 2)."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978645943294868*^9, 3.97864603904847*^9}, {
  3.978646193604847*^9, 3.9786462612918663`*^9}, {3.978738915564204*^9, 
  3.978738919198637*^9}, {3.978791842137179*^9, 
  3.978791845890905*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"09d42c70-f660-41d6-91eb-\
a15c4faa3028"],

Cell["\<\
The goal of this notebook is to apply \[ScriptCapitalU]\[ScriptCapitalD] to \
these wallpaper gauge fields, encoding transformation laws, invariants, and \
conserved quantities with the same rigor we applied to GR.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978645943294868*^9, 3.978646049988058*^9}, {
  3.9786462670246687`*^9, 3.9786462749793463`*^9}, {3.978646305201108*^9, 
  3.978646320622986*^9}, {3.9787918538270893`*^9, 
  3.978791855215496*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"e45636ff-3d1e-4a21-ac6b-\
fcb178545af0"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["A New CD", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978646468171488*^9, 
  3.978646472312533*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"c8c2732a-482b-40dd-ad69-\
34c9e0e28e1c"],

Cell["\<\
This new overload of CD can compute gauge connections (sections of the \
principal bundle of Appendix 2).\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978795270483778*^9, 3.978795277854451*^9}, {
  3.978795415257291*^9, 
  3.9787954500045643`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"9e34077f-ed2e-4587-87ba-\
5f5fa8adeb8f"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Gauge Covariant Derivative", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978795468371107*^9, 
  3.978795488321023*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"94acbd51-3b62-4f9c-a4df-\
f9e4ff0aa53d"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Inputs", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.978795503910904*^9, 
